          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have osteoarthritis in both knees.",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a long-term condition in which the cartilage that cushions a joint gradually wears down. When it affects both knees, it is still managed mainly in the same way: the knees are treated as joints that need to stay strong, mobile, and active. (Source: General clinical reference, *Osteoarthritis*; Rheumatology and orthopedic society guidance.)\n\n**What contributes to it:** Age, past joint injury or surgery, body weight, muscle weakness, joint shape and alignment, and genetic factors all play a part. Women are more affected after menopause. The reference notes the condition is manageable over many years and does not inevitably lead to joint replacement.\n\n**How it is classified:** In ICD-10-CM, the US coding system, osteoarthritis falls in the block M15 to M19. Codes in this block are divided first by joint, then by cause, and then by side (right, left, bilateral, or unspecified). I could not retrieve the exact code for osteoarthritis of both knees, so please check the specific code with your clinician or medical records.\n\n**Management in outline:** The guidance describes exercise as the core treatment. This combines muscle strengthening (especially the thigh and hip muscles), range-of-movement work, and aerobic activity. Weight management is also part of care where relevant. The guidance says exercise should ideally begin with some supervision so the movements and loads are right. Among medicines, the guidance lists topical anti-inflammatory creams as the preferred first option for knee osteoarthritis. It describes oral anti-inflammatories, steroid injections, and duloxetine as options for particular situations, and says these work alongside exercise rather than replacing it. It advises against opioids and against glucosamine and chondroitin. Any medicine decision should be made with a clinician, who can weigh your own health history.\n\nI could not retrieve the lifestyle-specific guidance on diet, sleep, or daily activity, so I cannot say more about those areas here. A physiotherapist or your clinician can help you plan an exercise program and talk through options that suit your knees.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
